# ── UK DOORWAY GRID RETIREMENT (2026-08-03) ─────────────────────────────────
# Supersedes the 2026-05-27 platform-route deprecation that used to live here.
#
# WHAT IS BEING RETIRED
# The 15 dynamic cities x 6 services grid at /uk/{city}/{service}, served from
# src/app/uk/[city]/[service]/page.tsx (route now deleted). Measured in the
# 2026-08-03 rendered audit: 620 rendered words each, and a word-level diff
# between any two of them differs in 26 words out of 620, every one the city
# name. That is the shape Google's spam policy calls scaled content abuse.
# GSC 2026-05-05..2026-08-02: the whole /uk/ tree earned 15 clicks and these
# 90 pages earned 5 of them. See docs/AI-SEO-RULEBOOK.md section 8.
#
# WHY THE CITY ROOT AND NOT THE NATIONAL HUB
# The obvious target was the matching national hub (/uk/seo, /uk/web-design and
# so on). Measured on live rendered HTML 2026-08-03, that is the wrong call:
#   - 10 of the 15 retiring cities (brighton, cambridge, cardiff, coventry,
#     derby, hull, leicester, oxford, plymouth, southampton) appear ZERO times
#     on any of the 10 UK hubs. A visitor 301'd from /uk/leicester/seo would
#     land on a page that never says "Leicester".
#   - The 15 city ROOT pages are being kept, and their ONLY in-content inbound
#     links are from the 6 doorway pages each. Sending the doorways to a hub
#     would orphan 10 kept pages the day it ships.
#   - `ai-websites` has no hub at all. /uk/ai-websites is a 404, and the terms
#     "ai website", "ai websites", "ai-powered website" and "ai web design"
#     each occur 0 times across all 10 hubs. There is nothing to point it at.
# Redirecting to /uk/{city} preserves the location intent exactly, makes the
# kept city roots the equity destination instead of stripping their only
# inbound links, and dissolves the ai-websites problem entirely.
# Caveat, recorded honestly: the city roots are ~970 words with no FAQ, so this
# consolidates thin pages into a less-thin page, not into a strong one.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES
#   - The 21 city ROOT pages /uk/{city}. They are the destination here.
#   - The two bespoke city x service pages /uk/london/web-design and
#     /uk/london/seo, which are hand-built static segments, not grid output.
#     They are NOT in the list below and must never be added to it.
#
# WHY EXPLICIT RULES INSTEAD OF /uk/:city/:service
# A placeholder rule would also swallow /uk/london/web-design and /uk/london/seo.
# Cloudflare's own docs say "redirects are always followed, regardless of whether
# or not an asset matches the incoming request", so a placeholder here would kill
# two live pages. The explicit rules cannot misfire, so they stay.
# CORRECTION (2026-08-04): this block used to claim they "cost nothing against
# the 2,100 static rule limit". That was wrong and it is what broke the file.
# The binding constraint is the 8 KiB rule-byte budget (see header) and this
# block is the single largest consumer of it. If budget runs out, reclaim it
# elsewhere first; do NOT collapse these into a placeholder.
# COLLAPSED 2026-08-04: these were 90 explicit {city}/{service} rules (~3.4 KB).
# All 90 mapped {city}/{service} -> /uk/{city} 301, verified uniform before the
# collapse, and none of the 15 cities has any live sub-page. One splat per city
# is exactly equivalent and also absorbs the /{service}/{platform} tier.
# London is deliberately NOT here: /uk/london/web-design and /uk/london/seo are
# real pages and a splat would kill them. Never add a london splat.
/uk/brighton/* /uk/brighton 301
/uk/bristol/* /uk/bristol 301
/uk/cambridge/* /uk/cambridge 301
/uk/cardiff/* /uk/cardiff 301
/uk/coventry/* /uk/coventry 301
/uk/derby/* /uk/derby 301
/uk/edinburgh/* /uk/edinburgh 301
/uk/glasgow/* /uk/glasgow 301
/uk/hull/* /uk/hull 301
/uk/leicester/* /uk/leicester 301
/uk/newcastle/* /uk/newcastle 301
/uk/nottingham/* /uk/nottingham 301
/uk/oxford/* /uk/oxford 301
/uk/plymouth/* /uk/plymouth 301
/uk/southampton/* /uk/southampton 301

# ── UK PLATFORM TIER, RE-POINTED (originally 2026-05-27, re-pointed 2026-08-03)
# The /uk/{city}/{service}/{platform} tier (~450 URLs) has 301'd to
# /uk/{city}/{service} since 2026-05-27. Now that the middle tier itself
# redirects, that rule would become a 2-hop chain, so each platform URL is
# re-pointed at the SAME final destination its middle tier goes to. Every UK
# redirect stays single-hop.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the two london rules must stay above the :city rule, because
# the topmost matching rule wins and london's middle tier is a real page.
/uk/london/web-design/:platform /uk/london/web-design 301
/uk/london/seo/:platform /uk/london/seo 301
/uk/:city/:service/:platform /uk/:city 301

